te for taking risk in the  industrial age the west very much had  the advantage they were the most they  had the most powerful militaries  um are you worried that that's changing  you know that that actually there is a  risk that  the this western dominance isn't by  right and actually could be  um overmatched by uh countries like  china developing  a technological advantage that could  then mean that they would be able to  just  you know outmatch us in all different  domains  uh definitely um i mean i think we see  that i mean china is a  an extraordinary country for innovation  and technological innovation for that  matter  and of course there is always um in  the character of warfare a constant  competition  between opposing technologies to try and  get match or overmatch  so the answer is yes that is definitely  something that needs to be  understood and dealt with you talk about  how like so the armed forces  obviously they exist to defend the  nation  um and and our allies and our interests  um but when it's like the the nation's  minds  that are under attack and when it's the  the institutions the pillars  of our democracy so the judiciary the  press  um that our government are our freedom  to elect  and our freedom to speak when it's that  that's being undermined  in the gray zone how can the military  help to defend that um  well first thing to recognize of course  is that the military can only play a  supporting role in this  because ultimately it's going to take  other government departments and other  instruments of statecraft  to protect it and i think all we can  really do  is to make sure that our part in all of  that  is as professionally delivered as it  possibly can be and that we are very  clear  a point the secretary state is often  making about the threat and you know we  do act as  a conscience as one would do as part of  the nation's  insurance policy to make sure people  understand the threat  and then clearly it's up to us to lobby  to make 